h support and  alignment in order to get the approval  to work on things but if you could just  like make the thing and then show to  people why it's good people are a lot  easily convinced that way that it is a  good idea and so if you are kind of  someone who is not great at gathering  support and alignment which let me tell  you that's a really hard thing then just  do the thing and then see what happens  there it's very tricky to know like how  to do things without stepping over a  line which really involves you needing  to know where that line is so like talk  to your manager about those lines what  those boundaries are who's responsible  for what what you can help out on get a  second opinion before you cross that  boundary a little bit and do things  without permission from another person  to see if it's a good idea a lot of  different ways without being like super  aggro about it the fourth hidden secret  is that communicating your limits  doesn't mean that you are weak or  unskilled so it's really hard to  communicate your limits because i feel  like in tech there's this kind of like  more is better do the most work that you  can the best work that you can push  yourself to your limit grind hustle  culture etc etc but the reality is we  all have limits we have limits on how  much work we can do how much energy we  have for that work and that's totally  okay i used to think that like by saying  that you can't do something or that like  you have a limit to something it means  that you're weak or that you're not able  to do that that's not true it just means  that you have boundaries and you have  limits and that's totally human and to  recognize it it's a lot better slash  like people like that more  than saying that you can deliver  something or saying that you can do  something and then not doing it by  communicating those boundaries people  can have the correct expectations for  you and then if you end up like over  exceeding then they're like pleasantly  